Worked engineering example
Unstructured discussion: “The new queue library is cursed. It keeps breaking production, so we should roll it back immediately and never use it again.”

Collapse stage
Team translation
Narrative
Remove “cursed” and the permanent technology verdict.
Evidence
Establish which queues, job types, deployments, and error signatures changed.
Urgency
Preserve immediate customer-impact containment; do not infer that every instance needs a permanent rollback.
Identity
Remove “the library choice proves the team made a bad decision.”
Symbolic
Replace “never use it again” with a decision based on measured failure modes and alternatives.

Observation: Job completion dropped from [baseline] after deployment [version]; workers show [error signature].
Hypothesis: The new library's acknowledgement behavior contributes to retries.
What remains: Customers are waiting on jobs; reliable processing is the priority.
Invariant: Grounded engineering agency.
Next safe action: Roll back or disable the affected path using the established control, preserve logs, and compare acknowledgement traces before declaring root cause.
Owner and check-in: [incident lead] / after [next trace sample].

The method does not prevent a rollback. It makes the rollback proportionate, auditable, and compatible with a later root-cause analysis.

Facilitation practices that keep it healthy
The team should use the method as a shared standard of clarity, not as a rhetorical weapon. A reviewer should never say “collapse your claim” to dismiss a colleague. Instead, use collaborative language such as: “Can we separate the observed behavior from the predicted risk?” or “What test would help us decide?”

Good practice
Avoid
Apply the same evidence standard to senior and junior contributors.
Using the method to override people with less status.
Preserve stated concerns and values in the written record.
Treating uncertainty as proof that the concern is irrational.
Time-box exploration and assign the next check.
Repeating the framework until a decision is avoided.
Escalate genuine security, safety, or policy concerns to the right owner.
Using “reversible” as a reason to ignore required controls.
Record decisions and assumptions in PRs or ADRs.
Reopening settled decisions without new evidence.

A two-week adoption plan
Begin with a small experiment rather than a policy mandate. During the first week, team leads model the template in one PR, one triage session, and one design discussion. The goal is simply to make the distinction between evidence and hypothesis visible.

During the second week, add the four review labels (fact, risk, question, preference) to the PR template and ask reviewers to use the full evidence block only for blocking comments or disagreements. At the end of the week, spend ten minutes in the retrospective asking whether the protocol made decisions clearer, faster, or fairer—and remove any step that became ceremonial rather than useful.

